**The Problem with ==:**
```java
// WRONG
String s1 = "Hello";
String s2 = "Hello";
if (s1 == s2) { }  // Works here but unreliable!

// WRONG - Fails with new String()
String s1 = new String("Hello");
String s2 = new String("Hello");
if (s1 == s2) {  // FALSE! Different objects
    System.out.println("Equal");
}
```

**Why == Doesn't Work:**
- == compares memory addresses (references)
- Two different String objects with same content have different addresses
- String pool behavior makes it work sometimes (confusing!)

**Correct Way - Use .equals():**
```java
// CORRECT
String s1 = new String("Hello");
String s2 = new String("Hello");
if (s1.equals(s2)) {  // TRUE! Compares content
    System.out.println("Equal");
}
```

**Different Scenarios:**

**1. Literal Strings (String Pool):**
```java
String s1 = "Hello";  // String pool
String s2 = "Hello";  // Same reference from pool
if (s1 == s2) { }     // TRUE (same object in pool)
if (s1.equals(s2)) { } // TRUE (same content)
```

**2. New String Objects:**
```java
String s1 = new String("Hello");  // New object
String s2 = new String("Hello");  // Different object
if (s1 == s2) { }                 // FALSE (different addresses)
if (s1.equals(s2)) { }            // TRUE (same content)
```

**3. Case-Insensitive Comparison:**
```java
String s1 = "Hello";
String s2 = "hello";

if (s1.equals(s2)) { }            // FALSE (case matters)
if (s1.equalsIgnoreCase(s2)) { }  // TRUE (ignores case)
```

**4. Null Safety:**
```java
// WRONG - Throws NullPointerException
String name = null;
if (name.equals("John")) { }  // CRASH!

// CORRECT - Literal first (safe)
if ("John".equals(name)) { }  // FALSE (no crash)

// CORRECT - Null check first
if (name != null && name.equals("John")) { }  // Safe
```

**5. Not Equal:**
```java
String input = "no";

// WRONG
if (input != "yes") { }  // Compares references!

// CORRECT
if (!input.equals("yes")) { }  // Compares content
```

**Memory Diagram:**
```
String Pool:              Heap:
┌─────────┐              ┌─────────┐
│ "Hello" │              │ "Hello" │
└────┬────┘              └────┬────┘
     │                        │
   s1, s2                    s3
(== works)              (== fails)
```

**Best Practices:**
✅ Always use .equals() for strings
✅ Use .equalsIgnoreCase() when case doesn't matter
✅ Put known non-null value first: "literal".equals(variable)
✅ Check for null before comparison when needed

❌ Never use == for string comparison
❌ Never use != for string comparison

