**Overflow - Exceeding Maximum Value:**

```java
// Byte overflow
byte maxByte = 127;  // Maximum byte value
System.out.println(maxByte);  // 127
maxByte++;
System.out.println(maxByte);  // -128 (wraps around!)

// Integer overflow
int maxInt = Integer.MAX_VALUE;  // 2,147,483,647
System.out.println(maxInt);  // 2147483647
System.out.println(maxInt + 1);  // -2147483648 (overflow!)
```

**Underflow - Going Below Minimum Value:**

```java
// Byte underflow
byte minByte = -128;  // Minimum byte value
System.out.println(minByte);  // -128
minByte--;
System.out.println(minByte);  // 127 (wraps around!)

// Integer underflow
int minInt = Integer.MIN_VALUE;  // -2,147,483,648
System.out.println(minInt);  // -2147483648
System.out.println(minInt - 1);  // 2147483647 (underflow!)
```

**Why It Happens:**
- Java uses **wraparound** behavior (modular arithmetic)
- Values wrap from max to min or min to max
- No error or exception is thrown!
- Silent failure can cause bugs

**Dangerous Example:**
```java
// Calculating large values
int million = 1000000;
int billion = million * 1000;  // Overflow! Wrong result
System.out.println(billion);  // Prints unexpected value

// CORRECT - Use long
long million = 1000000L;
long billion = million * 1000;  // Correct!
System.out.println(billion);  // 1000000000
```

**Range Limits:**
```java
System.out.println("byte: " + Byte.MIN_VALUE + " to " + Byte.MAX_VALUE);
// byte: -128 to 127

System.out.println("int: " + Integer.MIN_VALUE + " to " + Integer.MAX_VALUE);
// int: -2147483648 to 2147483647

System.out.println("long: " + Long.MIN_VALUE + " to " + Long.MAX_VALUE);
// long: -9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807
```

**How to Prevent:**

**1. Use Larger Data Type:**
```java
// Instead of int, use long
long bigNumber = 10000000000L;  // Too big for int
```

**2. Check Before Operations:**
```java
int a = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
if (a < Integer.MAX_VALUE) {  // Check before increment
    a++;
} else {
    System.out.println("Cannot increment - would overflow");
}
```

**3. Use Math Methods (Java 8+):**
```java
try {
    int result = Math.addExact(Integer.MAX_VALUE, 1);
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Overflow detected!");
}
```

**Real-World Impact:**
```java
// Financial calculation overflow
int accountBalance = 2000000000;  // $2 billion
int deposit = 500000000;          // $500 million
int newBalance = accountBalance + deposit;  // OVERFLOW! Negative!
System.out.println(newBalance);  // Shows negative (wrong!)

// CORRECT - Use long for money
long accountBalance = 2000000000L;
long deposit = 500000000L;
long newBalance = accountBalance + deposit;  // Correct!
System.out.println(newBalance);  // 2500000000
```

