**Default Values Table:**

| Type | Default Value |
|------|---------------|
| byte | 0 |
| short | 0 |
| int | 0 |
| long | 0L |
| float | 0.0f |
| double | 0.0d |
| char | '\u0000' |
| boolean | false |
| Reference (String, Object, Array) | null |

**Local Variables - NO Default Values:**

```java
public void method() {
    int count;  // NOT initialized

    // System.out.println(count);  // ERROR! Variable not initialized

    count = 0;  // Must initialize before use
    System.out.println(count);  // OK now
}
```

**Comparison:**

```java
public class DefaultValuesDemo {
    // Instance variable - has default value
    int instanceVar;  // Automatically 0

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Local variable - NO default value
        int localVar;  // NOT initialized

        DefaultValuesDemo obj = new DefaultValuesDemo();
        System.out.println(obj.instanceVar);  // 0 (OK)

        // System.out.println(localVar);  // ERROR!

        localVar = 10;  // Initialize first
        System.out.println(localVar);  // 10 (OK now)
    }
}
```

**Why the Difference?**

**Instance Variables:**
- Belong to objects
- Initialized when object is created
- Get default values automatically
- Stored in heap memory

**Local Variables:**
- Declared inside methods
- No automatic initialization
- Must be explicitly initialized
- Stored in stack memory
- Compiler error if used before initialization

**Common Mistakes:**

