**Variable Scope - Where Variables Can Be Accessed:**

**1. Local Scope (Method Variables):**
```java
public void method() {
    int localVar = 10;  // Scope: inside method only
    System.out.println(localVar);  // OK
}
// localVar does not exist here
```

**2. Instance Scope (Class Fields):**
```java
public class Student {
    int age = 20;  // Instance variable - class scope

    public void display() {
        System.out.println(age);  // Accessible in all methods
    }

    public void update() {
        age = 25;  // Can access and modify
    }
}
```

**3. Block Scope (Inside { }):**
```java
public void test() {
    if (true) {
        int blockVar = 5;  // Scope: inside if block only
        System.out.println(blockVar);  // OK
    }
    // System.out.println(blockVar);  // ERROR! Out of scope

    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
        int loopVar = i;  // Scope: inside loop only
    }
    // System.out.println(i);  // ERROR! Out of scope
}
```

**Complete Example:**
```java
public class ScopeDemo {
    // Instance variable - class scope
    int instanceVar = 100;

    public void method1() {
        // Local variable - method scope
        int localVar = 10;

        // Block scope
        {
            int blockVar = 20;
            System.out.println(instanceVar);  // 100 (OK)
            System.out.println(localVar);     // 10 (OK)
            System.out.println(blockVar);     // 20 (OK)
        }

        System.out.println(instanceVar);  // 100 (OK)
        System.out.println(localVar);     // 10 (OK)
        // System.out.println(blockVar);  // ERROR! Out of scope
    }

    public void method2() {
        System.out.println(instanceVar);  // 100 (OK)
        // System.out.println(localVar);  // ERROR! Different method
    }
}
```

**Variable Shadowing:**
```java
public class Shadowing {
    int count = 10;  // Instance variable

    public void method() {
        int count = 20;  // Local variable shadows instance
        System.out.println(count);      // 20 (local)
        System.out.println(this.count); // 10 (instance)
    }
}
```

**Scope Rules:**

**1. Inner can access outer:**
```java
int outer = 10;
{
    int inner = 20;
    System.out.println(outer);  // OK - inner can access outer
}
```

**2. Outer cannot access inner:**
```java
{
    int inner = 20;
}
// System.out.println(inner);  // ERROR! Outer cannot access inner
```

**3. Variables exist only in their scope:**
```java
if (true) {
    int x = 5;
}  // x is destroyed here

if (true) {
    int x = 10;  // OK - new variable, different scope
}
```

**Loop Variable Scope:**
```java
// i only exists inside loop
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
// System.out.println(i);  // ERROR! i out of scope

// Can declare same variable in different loops
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}
for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {  // OK - different scope
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

**Scope Lifetime:**
```java
public void example() {
    int a = 1;       // Created
    {                // New scope
        int b = 2;   // Created
        // a and b exist here
    }                // b destroyed
    // Only a exists here
}                    // a destroyed
```

**Best Practices:**
- Declare variables in smallest scope needed
- Avoid shadowing (same name in nested scopes)
- Use descriptive names to avoid scope confusion
- Minimize variable lifetime for better memory management

